1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,317
Section 9.1's Wednesday step says "generate whatever Tuesday's decisions require."

2
00:00:05,317 --> 00:00:07,194
That sentence hides a real gap.

3
00:00:07,194 --> 00:00:12,101
Deciding you need a new angle is not the same as knowing what that angle actually

4
00:00:12,101 --> 00:00:16,764
is, and staring at a blank brief on a Wednesday is where a lot of sprint time

5
00:00:16,764 --> 00:00:17,552
quietly goes.

6
00:00:17,552 --> 00:00:20,277
A swipe file closes that gap before it opens.

7
00:00:20,277 --> 00:00:24,881
It's a running collection of angles, hooks, and reference creative, gathered

8
00:00:24,881 --> 00:00:29,423
continuously rather than assembled from scratch under Wednesday's deadline.

9
00:00:29,423 --> 00:00:34,330
When Tuesday's decision says "new angle needed," you're choosing from a list, not

10
00:00:34,330 --> 00:00:35,904
inventing one on the spot.

11
00:00:35,904 --> 00:00:37,297
This isn't a new skill.

12
00:00:37,297 --> 00:00:42,022
Section 2.3 already taught you the hook taxonomy that makes a swipe file worth

13
00:00:42,022 --> 00:00:42,506
keeping.

14
00:00:42,506 --> 00:00:47,110
This lecture is about building the habit of feeding it continuously, so that

15
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:50,199
taxonomy has somewhere real to live between briefs.

16
00:00:50,199 --> 00:00:54,863
Why does this matter enough for its own lecture, rather than a single line in

17
00:00:54,863 --> 00:00:55,589
Section 9.1?

18
00:00:55,589 --> 00:01:00,132
Because the gap it closes is invisible until you're actually staring at it.

19
00:01:00,132 --> 00:01:04,857
A sprint that runs perfectly for three weeks can still stall completely on the

20
00:01:04,857 --> 00:01:09,763
fourth, the moment Tuesday calls for a genuinely new angle and nothing's ready to

21
00:01:09,763 --> 00:01:10,247
meet it.

22
00:01:10,797 --> 00:01:12,152
Notice the fourth row.

23
00:01:12,152 --> 00:01:15,293
Most swipe files only collect things worth copying.

24
00:01:15,293 --> 00:01:19,912
This one deliberately keeps a few things worth avoiding too, an overclaimed

25
00:01:19,912 --> 00:01:25,086
competitor ad, an obvious AI tell, because remembering what failure looks like is as

26
00:01:25,086 --> 00:01:27,180
useful as remembering what worked.

27
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:30,136
The first row is the one that compounds fastest.

28
00:01:30,136 --> 00:01:35,309
Every winner Section 8.5's scorecard confirms is worth adding here, not just logging

29
00:01:35,309 --> 00:01:36,233
and forgetting.

30
00:01:36,233 --> 00:01:41,222
A year into running this sprint, your own proven angles become the single richest

31
00:01:41,222 --> 00:01:45,841
source in the file, richer than anything borrowed from outside the account.

32
00:01:45,841 --> 00:01:50,891
Tag entries by awareness level as you add them, the same ladder Section 2.2 built.

33
00:01:50,891 --> 00:01:55,818
A swipe file sorted this way answers Wednesday's actual question directly: given

34
00:01:55,818 --> 00:02:00,992
this angle map's gaps, what's already sitting in the file for a cold audience versus

35
00:02:00,992 --> 00:02:01,669
a warm one.

36
00:02:01,669 --> 00:02:05,611
The second and third rows work together in a way worth noticing.

37
00:02:05,611 --> 00:02:09,860
Competitor and category ads show you what the wider market is trying.

38
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:14,910
Your own hook taxonomy tells you which pattern that ad is actually an instance of.

39
00:02:14,910 --> 00:02:19,837
Read together, they turn "I saw an ad I liked" into "that's a curiosity-gap hook

40
00:02:19,837 --> 00:02:24,641
aimed at a cold audience," which is the form Wednesday's brief actually needs.

41
00:02:25,191 --> 00:02:30,025
The first half of this habit happens whenever it happens, on a train, scrolling a

42
00:02:30,025 --> 00:02:33,128
competitor's feed, mid-conversation with a customer.

43
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:34,441
Capture takes seconds.

44
00:02:34,441 --> 00:02:39,036
The one-line tag is deliberate: full detail later, on Wednesday, when there's

45
00:02:39,036 --> 00:02:41,244
actually time to develop it properly.

46
00:02:41,244 --> 00:02:45,541
The second half is what makes Wednesday faster, not just more organised.

47
00:02:45,541 --> 00:02:50,375
Checking the file before opening a blank brief means the default question becomes

48
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:55,268
"does something here fit" rather than "what should I make," and the first question

49
00:02:55,268 --> 00:02:57,297
is almost always faster to answer.

50
00:02:57,297 --> 00:03:00,520
Notice this doesn't replace Section 2's brief process.

51
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:01,236
It feeds it.

52
00:03:01,236 --> 00:03:06,189
A swipe file entry becomes the seed of a proper brief, run through the same product

53
00:03:06,189 --> 00:03:08,874
truth doc and hook taxonomy as anything else.

54
00:03:08,874 --> 00:03:13,887
It just means that seed already exists on Wednesday morning instead of needing to be

55
00:03:13,887 --> 00:03:15,319
invented under deadline.

56
00:03:15,319 --> 00:03:17,468
Where should you actually keep this?

57
00:03:17,468 --> 00:03:20,213
Anywhere you'll genuinely open on a Wednesday.

58
00:03:20,213 --> 00:03:25,166
A shared document, a simple spreadsheet, even a folder of screenshots with one-line

59
00:03:25,166 --> 00:03:25,703
captions.

60
00:03:25,703 --> 00:03:30,716
The specific tool matters far less than whether it's somewhere you'll actually check

61
00:03:30,716 --> 00:03:35,430
before reaching for a blank brief, which is the entire reason this file exists.

62
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:40,953
One honest trap worth naming, because a well-stocked swipe file makes it genuinely

63
00:03:40,953 --> 00:03:41,499
tempting.

64
00:03:41,499 --> 00:03:46,351
Studying why a competitor's hook works, the structure, the pacing, the awareness

65
00:03:46,351 --> 00:03:49,626
level it's aimed at, is exactly what this file is for.

66
00:03:49,626 --> 00:03:54,600
Copying their actual claim word for word is a different act entirely, and it skips

67
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:57,935
straight past Section 6's entire pre-flight discipline.

68
00:03:57,935 --> 00:04:02,484
You have no idea whether that claim would survive your own product's claims

69
00:04:02,484 --> 00:04:06,790
boundary, because it was never tested against yours in the first place.

70
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:09,519
Keep the two separate in how you tag entries.

71
00:04:09,519 --> 00:04:14,493
Note the pattern that caught your attention, never the exact line, and rebuild the

72
00:04:14,493 --> 00:04:17,465
claim from your own product truth doc every time.

73
00:04:17,465 --> 00:04:19,041
The structure is reusable.

74
00:04:19,041 --> 00:04:21,649
The specific words underneath it never are.

75
00:04:21,649 --> 00:04:26,259
This is the same discipline Section 6.2 already built around Harlan's killed

76
00:04:26,259 --> 00:04:28,867
swelling claim, aimed at a new entry point.

77
00:04:28,867 --> 00:04:33,779
A borrowed claim never even reaches a human sign-off gate to catch it, because it

78
00:04:33,779 --> 00:04:36,873
looks like it's already been proven somewhere else.

79
00:04:36,873 --> 00:04:37,479
It hasn't.

80
00:04:37,479 --> 00:04:42,574
It's only been proven on a product, an audience, and a legal footing that were never

81
00:04:42,574 --> 00:04:43,787
yours to begin with.

82
00:04:44,337 --> 00:04:49,182
Next lecture turns everything this section has built, the sprint, the economics,

83
00:04:49,182 --> 00:04:53,421
this file, into a report someone outside the work can actually follow.

84
00:04:53,421 --> 00:04:56,994
Before that, start the file today, even with three entries.

85
00:04:56,994 --> 00:05:01,839
An angle you noticed this week, a hook pattern from Section 2.3 you haven't used

86
00:05:01,839 --> 00:05:03,535
yet, one cautionary example.

87
00:05:03,535 --> 00:05:07,774
Three entries beats an empty folder the next time Wednesday needs one.

88
00:05:07,774 --> 00:05:12,498
Revisit it at the end of every sprint, too, not only when you're adding to it.

89
00:05:12,498 --> 00:05:17,403
A swipe file nobody prunes eventually turns into noise, dozens of half-remembered

90
00:05:17,403 --> 00:05:20,552
entries with no clear reason any of them were saved.

91
00:05:20,552 --> 00:05:25,397
Five minutes a week, deciding what's still worth keeping and what's quietly gone

92
00:05:25,397 --> 00:05:29,152
stale, keeps the file genuinely useful rather than just large.
